" (https://www.parconazionale5terre.it/pdf/Liguria-5Terre.pdf)\nciteturn41view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 8; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.parconazionale5terre.it/pdf/Liguria-5Terre.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 264\nL0@P0: Writers: Marcello Cappellazzi and Valentina Pinzuti are independent contributors to Revolve.\nL1@P0: Photographer: Arianna Pagani\nL2@P0: LIGURIA:\nL3@P0: Preserving the Land\nL4@P0: THIS\nL5@P0: FEATURE IS PART OF\nL6@P0: REVOLVE WATER\nL7@P0: www.revolve-water.com\nL8@P0-1: COMING SOON!Nestled on the scenic northern Mediterranean coast in the Region\nL9@P1: of Liguria, the Cinque Terre (Five Lands) – Monterosso al Mare,\nL10@P1: Vernazza, Corniglia, Manarola, Riomaggiore – are renowned as an\nL11@P1: exquisite tourist destination. In 1997, UNESCO World Heritage listed\nL12@P1: Cinque Terre as a “living cultural landscape” and in 1999 the cluster\nL13@P1: of five villages were declared a national park. Of the five medieval\nL14@P1: fishing villages, Monterosso al Mare and Vernazza were devastated\nL15@P1: by flash floods in October 2011. Revolve visited the villages in June\nL16@P1-2: 2013 to see the reconstruction efforts.78\nL17@P2: lenges inherent to the Ligurian region.\nL18@P2: For the inhabitants, it is essential to\nL19@P2: maintain an alignment with the\nL20@P2: traditional ways of life while mak\u0002ing provisions to alleviate and\nL21@P2: lessen the amount of destruction\nL22@P2: should another flood occur. Bet\u0002ter prevention measures are key\nL23@P2: to properly addressing the threat\nL24@P2: of flash floods in Cinque Terre as\nL25@P2: in other areas around the Mediter\u0002ranean and beyond.\nL26@P2: Geologist, Carlo Malgarotto, says, “this is\nL27@P2: an extremely dynamic environment where\nL28@P2: On the Italian north-west coast, in the prov\u0002ince of La Spezia, Liguria, little towns with\nL29@P2: colorful houses cling to steep hills along a\nL30@P2: narrow, rugged strip of land between the\nL31@P2: Maritime Alps, the Apennine Mountains\nL32@P2: and the Mediterranean Sea. The rich veg\u0002etation, scenic vineyards, hiking trails with\nL33@P2: spectacular views distinctively define these\nL34@P2: five medieval towns of Cinque Terre. Such\nL35@P2: morphology, while visually pleasing can\nL36@P2: have fragile land conditions, thus creating\nL37@P2: instability especially with heavy rains. Such\nL38@P2: was the case in late October 2011 when\nL39@P2: Cinque Terre was hit severely by heavy\nL40@P2-3: flooding which redefined the natural chal- Carlo Malgarotto. 79\nL41@P3: same territory, under emergency conditions,\nL42@P3: interventions can only be planned case by\nL43@P3: case. Only indepth research of the territory\nL44@P3: will provide the best approach for the con\u0002tainment of damages and risks.”\nL45@P3: One of the most dangerous risks of heavy\nL46@P3: precipitation is “detritus flow”, where the\nL47@P3: sheer inclination of the hills causes massive\nL48@P3: amounts of water to rush violently down,\nL49@P3: dislocating and destroying everything in its\nL50@P3: path. A complication encountered during the\nL51@P3: flooding was the lack of a sufficient relief\nL52@P3: valve for the water stream coming from the\nL53@P3: mountains. With drainage overwhelmed, the\nL54@P3: cement and asphalt channeled the water\nL55@P3: that accumulated and gushed destructively\nL56@P3: through villages, such as Vernazza.\nL57@P3: significant changes arise in a very brief time\nL58@P3: period and where the unusual morphological\nL59@P3: and meteorological conditions created by the\nL60@P3: natural architecture have been exacerbated\nL61@P3: in the last few years. The biggest challenge\nL62@P3: is that the area is not affected by the same\nL63@P3: risks in the same way. The fierce force of the\nL64@P3: heavy rains tends usually to concentrate only\nL65@P3: on a narrow portion of land and to barely\nL66@P3: affect some others. In October 2011, while\nL67@P3: the two villages of Monterosso al Mare and\nL68@P3: Vernazza were inundated by flooding, the\nL69@P3: water scarcely damaged the other three vil\u0002lages. With no homogenous dynamic at play\nL70@P3: and many geological differences within the\nL71@P3: Image page 76-77: View on Monterosso al Mare. Left page (top): The road to\nL72@P3: Vernazza is still closed. Left page (bottom): Poster showing the consequences of\nL73@P3-4: flood of 25th October 2011. Vernazza. This page: Monterosso al Mare.80\nL74@P4: from the town and much of the area was ren\u0002ovated. This crisis provided the opportunity to\nL75@P4: re-engineer the streets and sewer systems\nL76@P4: to prevent other potential disasters, and to\nL77@P4: remold the connection between the commu\u0002nity and the broader region. The result is a\nL78@P4: model for sustainable development based on\nL79@P4: the intrinsic value of Cinque Terre.\nL80@P4: In his office, Vincenzo Resasco, the Mayor\nL81@P4: of Vernazza, shows a video of a severe\nL82@P4: rainstorm that in October 2011 flooded the\nL83@P4: Cinque Terre towns of Vernazza and Mon\u0002terosso al Mare causing uncontrolled land\u0002slides. With emergency intervention aided\nL84@P4: by the local residents and relief workers of\nL85@P4: Vernazza, he removed rocks, mud and debris\nL86@P4: Special Focus: Vernazza\nL87@P4: Q&A: Vincenzo Resasco\nL88@P4: Mayor of Vernazza, Cinque Terre\nL89@P4: It took only nine months to restore\nL90@P4: Vernazza's city center after the\nL91@P4: disaster in October 2011. What\nL92@P4: emergency measures were taken\nL93@P4: and what long-term objectives\nL94@P4: were established?\nL95@P4: We had to work quickly and\nL96@P4: rebuild both the town’s security\nL97@P4: and its economic stability. I stayed\nL98@P4: in Vernazza with a group of 80\nL99@P4: volunteers from 7 November 2011\nL100@P4: to the end of March 2012. Everyone\nL101@P4: else was relocated. That was a tough\nL102@P4: winter, with many dangers due the hydro\u0002geological conditions and the snowfalls. We\nL103@P4: received most material to rehabilitate Ver\u0002nazza by sea, through the small town harbor.\nL104@P4: Supplies were stored in the church nearby.\nL105@P4: The question we asked ourselves after this\nL106@P4: experience was how we must envision our\nL107@P4: future. It is important to remember mistakes\nL108@P4: to better prepare for the future. It had to be\nL109@P4: a shared mission so we created a laboratory\nL110@P4: for technicians to cooperate with the staff\nL111@P4: from the region – the project is called Ver\u0002nazza Futura.\nL112@P4: Who were the participants?\nL113@P4: Citizens, public administrators, techni\u0002cians, entrepreneurs and tourism manag\u0002ers. This shared experience was struc\u0002tured around four main working groups:\nL114@P4: Quality of Life, Renewable Energy and\nL115@P4: Environment, Agriculture, and Tourism.\nL116@P4: After discussing these themes together,\nL117@P4: the participants set a series of priorities\nL118@P4: for action to address the needs.\nL119@P4: How else did you address\nL120@P4: hydro-geological risks and territorial\nL121@P4: management?\nL122@P4: There were all kinds of interventions in the\nL123@P4: wake of the emergency. The underground\nL124@P4: channels were reconstructed and new\nL125@P4: banks were built with reinforced concrete\nL126@P4: as we could not use stones for this purpose.\nL127@P4: There were interventions that had to be done\nL128@P4: immediately as people were still in danger.\nL129@P4: There are still three or four land areas at risk.\nL130@P4: We contacted the owners of the dry-stone\nL131@P4: walls to know who wanted to rebuild them.\nL132@P4: Some agreed; others not. They were just\nL133@P4: contained with iron nets. The government\nL134@P4: cannot intervene on privately owned land.\nL135@P4: With public money it is not always possible\nL136@P4: to invest on private land.\nL137@P4: Vincenzo Resasco\nL138@P4: Better prevention measures are key to\nL139@P4: properly addressing the threat of flash floods\nL140@P4: in Cinque Terre as in other areas around the\nL141@P4-5: Mediterranean and beyond.81\nL142@P5: October 2011 crumbled down on the Ver\u0002nazza and Monterosso.\nL143@P5: According to Laura Canale, Head of the\nL144@P5: European and International Affairs Office of\nL145@P5: Liguria Region, this has been an occasion to\nL146@P5: rethink the developmental model for Cinque\nL147@P5: Terre. Interventions on the publicly-owned\nL148@P5: lands of the Ente Parco have pointed in this\nL149@P5: direction by restoring and maintaining the\nL150@P5: traditional terraces and dry-stone walls in\nL151@P5: the Corniolo area, as well as by promoting a\nL152@P5: territory-based tourism.\nL153@P5: The challenge is now to expand this model\nL154@P5: to include privately-owned land. The Mayor\nL155@P5: of Vernazza clearly recognizes that the\nL156@P5: tourism economy in Cinque Terre must\nL157@P5: invest part of its resources in its territory for\nL158@P5: cultivation and maintenance. Many already\nL159@P5: work towards this goal: for example, the\nL160@P5: cooperative Cantina Sociale has been able\nL161@P5: to maintain a margin of profitability for\nL162@P5: farmers in Cinque Terre. The cooperative\nL163@P5: in fact buys grapes from its members at\nL164@P5: 2.50 €/Kg, while in other areas the usual\nL165@P5: price is 0.2 €/Kg. This difference reflects\nL166@P5: the higher costs a farmer in Cinque Terre\nL167@P5: faces both in terms of yields and labor to\nL168@P5: work on the steep hills.\nL169@P5: In Cinque Terre, most people maintain their\nL170@P5: land by building terraced plots comprising\nL171@P5: several thousand kilometers of dry-stone\nL172@P5: walls. The value of this unique landscape\nL173@P5: relies on the conservation of its fragile\nL174@P5: region and, for many years, this has been\nL175@P5: the duty of farmers cultivating the terraced\nL176@P5: vineyards. However, tourism has become a\nL177@P5: work alternative as a more lucrative way to\nL178@P5: make a living, thus leading to a progres\u0002sive reduction of cultivated areas. Land\nL179@P5: abandonment has become a plague for the\nL180@P5: territory that started to deteriorate and in\nL181@P5: Reconstruction Efforts\nL182@P5-6: Agriculture and Viticulture82\nL183@P6: to the trail that runs close to the coastline.\nL184@P6: During the high season there is much con\u0002gestion along the famous coastal trail (the\nL185@P6: “Via dell’amore” - Love Walk).\nL186@P6: As Francesco Galleni, President of ATI 5\nL187@P6: Terre, the consortium that took over man\u0002agement of the Cinque Terre National Park,\nL188@P6: explains, “European funding allows us to\nL189@P6: plan a massive recovery of the park, with\nL190@P6: the specific purpose of solving this incon\u0002venience by facilitating tourist access to the\nL191@P6: upper inland areas.” This will create favor\u0002able conditions for new economic develop\u0002ment activities, such as bed&breakfasts\nL192@P6: and rest stops, while stimulating economic\nL193@P6: growth with sound management inland.\nL194@P6: According to Maurizio Cattani, from the La\nL195@P6: Spezia section of the Italian Alpine Club,\nL196@P6: many efforts have also been made to clear\nL197@P6: and manage the upper and lateral hiking\nL198@P6: paths and in the development of applica\u0002tions for iOs and Android mobile systems\nL199@P6: to ensure comprehensive information on\nL200@P6: access, conditions and practicality of all\nL201@P6: the hiking trails. The long-term goal of\nL202@P6: this recovery plan has the added value of\nL203@P6: bringing investments in tourism, and to\nL204@P6: contribute to a sound management of the\nL205@P6: inland areas that is fundamental for the\nL206@P6: prevention of hydro-geological risk.\nL207@P6: Over the past decades, the Cinque Terre\nL208@P6: National Park has made many efforts to\nL209@P6: encourage responsible and eco-sustainable\nL210@P6: tourism that would respect the cultural\nL211@P6: value of the site. However, the flash flood\u0002ing of 2011 has raised some serious ques\u0002tioning on the future of tourism in Cinque\nL212@P6: Terre, highlighting the need to stretch this\nL213@P6: commitment even further. Due to its rug\u0002ged coast, the lack of long beaches and\nL214@P6: the numerous hiking trails, Cinque Terre\nL215@P6: National Park is generally a hiking destina\u0002tion. It has always attracted daily visitors\nL216@P6: but most tourists tend to confine their visit\nL217@P6: to the lower areas, to the five villages and\nL218@P6-7: Tourism and Woodlands83\nL219@P7: The Payments for Environmental Services\nL220@P7: (PES) is the economic instrument identi\u0002fied by SylvaMED for enhancing the value\nL221@P7: of Mediterranean forests. PES are designed\nL222@P7: to make the users of forest services and\nL223@P7: consumers of forest products financial\nL224@P7: contributors to the costs of providing and\nL225@P7: maintaining the forests, thereby generating\nL226@P7: cash flows. In Liguria, SylvaMED financed\nL227@P7: a project in the Polcevera Valley close to\nL228@P7: Genoa. Laura Muraglia, Head of the proj\u0002ect, stresses the importance of appropri\u0002ate forest management to protect against\nL229@P7: soil erosion and river flooding. The most\nL230@P7: important achievement, as demonstrated in\nL231@P7: Cinque Terre, is delivering the message to\nL232@P7: address and prevent hydro-geological and\nL233@P7: geo-morphological risks, while restructuring\nL234@P7: solid development models for marginalized\nL235@P7: and fragile regions.\nL236@P7: For more images on Liguria:\nL237@P7: www.revolve-water.com\nL238@P7: Industries and tourism operators have con\u0002centrated their development on the coast\u0002line, leaving the inlands marginalized and\nL239@P7: abandoned. In Liguria Region, 80% of the\nL240@P7: population lives on 20% of the land; how\u0002ever, the balance of its productive system\nL241@P7: strongly depends on the rural areas. Daniela\nL242@P7: Minetti from the Regional Agency for the\nL243@P7: Environment (ARPAL) explains that “main\u0002taining Liguria’s fragile ecosystems has\nL244@P7: become a priority not only for preserving its\nL245@P7: biodiversity and managing hydro-geological\nL246@P7: risks, but especially to renewing confidence\nL247@P7: within inland areas.”\nL248@P7: The SylvaMED Project explores the poten\u0002tial that forestry has for the development\nL249@P7: of rural areas in Liguria as well as in other\nL250@P7: regions around the Mediterranean. The\nL251@P7: recognition of ecosystem services varies\nL252@P7: significantly from country to country, but\nL253@P7: the Mediterranean forest is a special case\nL254@P7: given its economic and cultural importance.\nL255@P7: Land Management\nL256@P7: SylvaMED: the\nL257@P7: importance of forests in\nL258@P7: the Mediterranean\nL259@P7: “Cinque Terre is illustrative of the evolution of human society and\nL260@P7: settlement over time, under the influences of the physical constraints\nL261@P7: and/or opportunities presented by their natural environment [...]”\nL262@P7: – World Heritage Cultural Landscapes. A handbook for Conservation and Management. World Heritage Paper series #26, 1997\nL263@P7: Image (Pages 80-81): Dry-stone walls and terraces in Monterosso. This page: The Cinque Terre.\n"
